<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tambralinga</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 1300 CE (approx.) · capital Nakhon Si Thammarat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Buddhist trading kingdom on the Malay Peninsula centred on Nakhon Si Thammarat, whose king Chandrabhanu invaded Sri Lanka in the 13th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tambralinga appears in Chinese sources as Danmaling and in inscriptions, including a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> list of conquests of about 1030. Its centre lay at or near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Nakhon Si Thammarat</a> on the east coast of the peninsula. For long periods it was tied to the maritime network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, before asserting itself as an independent power.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom drew its wealth from the trans-peninsular trade and exports such as aromatic woods and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>. It sent missions to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> court in the 11th century. Its most famous ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandrabhanu">Chandrabhanu Sridhamaraja</a>, recorded in an inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaiya">Chaiya</a> dated 1230, built up a navy and twice invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, partly to obtain a sacred relic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandrabhanu">Chandrabhanu</a> was defeated in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> by Pandyan forces in the 1260s, and Tambralinga weakened. By the late 13th century it had become the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nakhon-si-thammarat-kingdom">Nakhon Si Thammarat Kingdom</a>, which accepted the overlordship of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, while retaining strong Sinhalese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> links.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tambralinga.jpg" alt="Tambralinga" width="320" height="157"><figcaption>Image: Ubs6u!d-pongsakorn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tambralinga_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 1300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nakhon Si Thammarat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chandrabhanu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Malay, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Danmaling, Tanmaling</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=950&amp;focus=tambralinga" aria-label="Tambralinga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7442 1863 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tambralinga lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7762 2119L7833 2434M7762 2119L7762 2119"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7833 2434h0M7762 2119h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7762 2119h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7762 2119h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nakhon Si Thammarat. <a href="/atlas?year=950&amp;focus=tambralinga">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
